course: dictionary
lesson: 4280
title: Greedily: taking more than you need
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «greedily» is an adverb, so it tells you how someone does an action. «The dog gobbled its dinner greedily, then looked up for more.» The dog did not just eat. It ate fast and wanted all of it, so greedily means in a greedy way.
explain: Greedily can also mean very eagerly, wanting something very much. «She read greedily, one book after another.» Nobody is stealing books here. The reader simply wants to read more and more.
explain: To work it out, look for clues: is someone eating, grabbing or looking at something they want? Is the person taking more than they need? Then swap in «in a greedy way» or «very eagerly» and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Scoff is a UK word that means eat greedily. «He scoffed his sandwich before the bell.» Scoff and greedily are close in meaning, but scoff is a more informal, everyday word, so a dictionary tells you it is a regional word.
fact: Greedily is an adverb, so it tells you how someone does an action.
fact: «Greedily» means in a greedy way, wanting or taking more than you need, especially food or money.
fact: «Greedily» can also mean very eagerly, wanting something very much, as in «reading greedily».
fact: Phrases to spot: eat greedily, look greedily at, grab greedily.
fact: «Scoff» (UK) means eat greedily, so it is a regional word for the same kind of eating.
exercise: «The fox snatched the chicken and ate it greedily.» What does greedily tell us? | In a calm, gentle way | *In a greedy way, wanting more than it needs | In a slow, careful way
exercise: «The children looked greedily at the cake.» What does looked greedily suggest? | They had already eaten it | They were bored by the cake | They were scared of the cake | *They wanted the cake very much
exercise: Ella says scoff is a UK word. What does scoff mean? | Sleep deeply | Walk slowly | Laugh loudly | *Eat greedily
test:
«She grabbed the last biscuit greedily.» Which word is closest in meaning to greedily here? | Politely | Gently | *Eagerly | Slowly
test:
«The reader turned the pages greedily, desperate to find out the ending.» What does greedily mean here? | Carelessly | *Very eagerly, wanting the story very much | Unwillingly | Sadly
exam:
«The greedy king took all the gold and grabbed greedily at every coin.» What does grabbed greedily show about the king? | Kind and generous | *Selfish, wanting far more than he needs | Tired and sleepy | Frightened of the coins
